  "after": "Reads share immutable metadata, hashes use compact strings, and review indexes skip irrelevant leaves. Undo and redo reuse node lookup storage. Layout keys avoid temporary attribute pairs, and cached table paragraphs skip break-option construction. Proven ordinary text edits reuse bounded drawing projections and source order. Unchanged page and row records retain their layout and painting data. Ordinary text edits reuse table formatting and column measurements when their dependencies match. Cache recency avoids repeated key insertion, and complete fitting text lines retain their measurements across compatible width changes. Ordinary rows reuse the latest compatible measurement during placement. Formatting reads share paragraph membership indexes. Formatting reads share one demand-loaded paragraph index across properties, tabs, indentation, and empty styles. Empty cell paragraphs retain their line boxes when the cell ignores its end marker height. AutoFit width changes retain compatible table geometry and painted content. Paragraph cache retention uses byte budgets, and bulk selection reads share layout indexes. Last table rows can grow within the paginator page band when row membership stays unchanged. Whole-subtree read caches omit small leaf groups. Ordered table borders and moved rows reuse proven layout facts. Cell spans share immutable ranges. Side-rule copies share width-independent row facts and compatible cell insets. Repeated header placements and stable merge-plan reads reuse exact results. Table geometry readers skip proven irrelevant fragments. Compatible split rows keep their page placements after width changes. Undo revisions share equal table tokens and unchanged break-key chunks. Formatting reads index requested paragraphs. Ordinary row growth replays exact vertical calculations. Full table pagination reuses compatible repeated header plans and placements. Paragraph splits and joins before unchanged tables reuse compatible row placements. Paragraph splits use the committed tail for caret placement. Table resizing uses existing unique node indexes and preserves horizontal grid spans. Plain contiguous left-to-right lines retain their span records.",
